    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d457">

                <pb facs="0635" n="511" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg511"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Ic/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ruddock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-15T19:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 15, 1921—7
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 15—1:54
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>262. Your telegram number 193, July 12th, 5 p.m. Diplomatic corps informed of
                    Department’s views. Ichang situation not deemed as critical as Urga, however
                    understand that a third looting <pb facs="0636" n="512" xml:id="pg512"/> there
                    narrowly avoided recently through the presence of several foreign gunboats.
                    While it is never possible forecast strength and location of mutinies which are
                    becoming increasingly common, it is not thought that warning should as yet be
                    given as in 1911.</p>
                <p>However, I am inquiring the opinion of Admiral Strauss<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d457-f1"/> who was recently in Ichang and shall report again.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ruddock</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d457-f1" n="4" xml:id="d457-f1-content">
                <p>Rear Admiral Joseph Strauss, Commander in Chief of the American Asiatic
                    Fleet.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
